From 57709199731cde6743038fb45c1fd192e26372c8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Owen <3221756+christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 21:31:02 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 507/507] [DS4.1] Candidate source: its candidates published as
 block ids

The candidate source layer (20) published, a row, the 16,384 positions of
the 2,048 blocks of 8 it keeps, and the reindex layers (24, 28, 32, 36) read
them back. Every kept block but the last is wholly visible, and the last
holds the row's last position (always kept, and the highest), so that list
is the blocks' positions with -1 only past its count: candidate j < count is
ids[j // 8] * 8 + j % 8. The source now writes the 2,048 block ids (-1
padded) and the count of positions; the reindex score kernel computes each
candidate's position from them (a multiple of the block plus an offset below
it, so its page arithmetic folds to the block id's), and its top-k maps the
entries it keeps through them. The candidates are the same, in the same
order, so every score and selection keeps its bits, and at the serving
shapes the reindex call is not slower (dgx4, against the position lists:
238.6 against 246.3 us).

The score kernel still reads no key past a row's visible positions (DeepSeek's
reference reads index_k[:, : end_pos // ratio]): every candidate's position is
a written one. A masked block reads block 0, whole in any row of more than one
block. Past the row's count the last block's ids point past the row's end, so
a candidate there takes offset 0: the last kept block's first position, or
position 0 past the kept blocks, as a position list's -1 reads position 0.
Only the tile holding the count needs that bound, so the kernel chooses once a
tile, and the tiles wholly below the count compute positions without it.
Bounding every load by the count instead cost the reindex call 4% (257.3
against 247.1 us), and bounding every candidate's offset 4-6%.

deepseek_v41_indexer_reads.py's candidate case, scored under
compute-sanitizer's initcheck, now passes block ids the way the source writes
them (the last block partly visible, one masked block), and keeps position
lists for a module without them (the release's positive control). The
indexer without the count's bound reports thousands of unwritten reads there.

The TileLang attention's candidate staging shrinks from 64 KiB to 8 KiB a
budget token on every rank (448 MiB less at TP3's 8,192 tokens, 896 MiB at
the ring's 16,384), sized by the indexer's CANDIDATE_BLOCKS.

Tests: the grouped top-k and the source plan against the reference in block
ids; reindex rows read block-id lists (a partly visible last block, a masked
block, an empty row); a chain test checks that the source's ids expand to the
release's position list exactly and that the reindex scores and selection
equal the reference over it; test_indexer_reads_only_live_pages's candidate
case (the page tables poisoned past each row's live pages) passes block ids
the same way. benchmark_deepseek_v41_candidate_ids.py runs the
position-list indexer and this one on the same inputs at the serving shapes
(1M positions, pages of 256) and gates on bit equality and no slowdown.
